README
======

jsrxe builds librxe for the browser, and a single-page application on top of
it that exposes what the library can do.

The engine is not reimplemented. librxe is compiled to WebAssembly with
Emscripten and used as it stands, because the thing this library sells is that
an index maps to an exact member and keeps doing so -- a key reproduces a run,
the enumeration order is part of the interface -- and a second implementation
that differed anywhere would be worse than no port at all. Only the user
interface is written in JavaScript.


WHAT GETS BUILT
===============

bin/rxenum.js       The demo program compiled whole, run under node.
                    bin/rxenum-wasm wraps it so that librxe's own test suite
                    can be pointed at this build unchanged:

                        cd ../rxe && RXENUM=../jsrxe/bin/rxenum-wasm make test

                    That is the real check on the port. It runs the 353 pinned
                    cases, the 66-pattern oracle and the shortlex brute-force
                    against the WebAssembly build, so the port is verified
                    rather than assumed. Nothing here reimplements the option
                    parsing or the output formatting, on purpose: this is
                    rxenum.c itself, so it cannot drift from it.

web/librxe.js       The library alone, with the binding in src/binding.c, for
                    the interface to drive. No option parsing, no formatting.
                    Indices cross the boundary as decimal strings, since they
                    routinely exceed what a double holds exactly, and a
                    decimal string round-trips through BigInt losslessly.

                    web/engine.js wraps it and knows about neither the worker
                    nor the page, so the same code is driven by the browser
                    and by tests/node.mjs. web/worker.js is a module worker
                    holding one instance of it; the page talks to that.

                    An example may carry a snippet of code, run on each
                    generated element and shown alongside it -- appending a
                    CPF or CNPJ check digit, hashing a candidate password.
                    web/sandbox.js compiles it in strict mode with no reach
                    into the surrounding scope and a small pure helper library
                    (sha256, mod11, checkDigits); web/sha256.js is a
                    synchronous SHA-256, since crypto.subtle is asynchronous
                    and absent from a file:// page. It is not a security
                    boundary in the strong sense, only enough that the
                    accidental case is safe and the interface small.


STATUS
======

Working. librxe's own suite passes in full against the WebAssembly build, and
the interface runs on top of it: a library of examples with notes, in English
and Brazilian Portuguese, exercising counting, seeking, the shuffle key, the
direction flag and random choice.

'make test' runs three things: librxe's suite through bin/rxenum-wasm, the
binding layer under node, and the whole page in a real browser.

Two things had to be dealt with, and both are worth knowing about.

The stack has to be raised from the default 64KB. print_grouped sizes a
variable-length array from the number of decimal digits it is about to write,
and this library's whole business is numbers with a great many of them: the
cardinality of '[a-z]{1,20000}' has 28,300, which overflows the stack and
traps with "table index is out of bounds".

And Emscripten's libc is musl, whose getopt stops at the first non-option
where the GNU one reorders argv. That turned up a real portability bug in
rxenum rather than anything to do with WebAssembly, and was fixed there.

A third thing is worth writing down because it wasted an hour: Chrome's
--virtual-time-budget does not advance timers inside a worker. A page whose
worker waits on any timer -- which every Emscripten module does while it
instantiates -- appears to hang under --dump-dom while being perfectly well
in a real browser. tests/browser.mjs therefore drives Chrome over the
DevTools protocol in real time instead.


REQUIREMENTS
============

Emscripten, from the official SDK; version 6.0.6 is what this was built with.
The Debian and Ubuntu package is 3.1.6, released in early 2022, and does not
work with Node 18 or later: its generated loader calls fetch() on a filesystem
path, and a main() taking argc and argv is not run at all. Install the SDK
instead, which needs no root:

    git clone https://github.com/emscripten-core/emsdk.git ~/emsdk
    cd ~/emsdk && ./emsdk install latest && ./emsdk activate latest
    . ~/emsdk/emsdk_env.sh

GMP is librxe's only dependency and there is no packaged build of it for
WebAssembly, so the Makefile fetches the source and builds it:

    make gmp

That needs curl, tar, xz and m4, and takes a few minutes. It is done once.


BUILDING
========

    make            builds both artefacts
    make bundle     builds dist/rxenum.html, one file that needs no server
    make test       runs everything: librxe's suite through the WebAssembly
                    build, the binding layer under node, and the page, the
                    single-file build and both in a real browser
    make serve      serves web/ on http://localhost:8000/web/


WHY IT NEEDS A SERVER
=====================

web/ has to be served over http, and will not work opened as a file. That is
not a shortcoming of the browser: a page opened from a file:// URL has the
opaque origin "null", and three separate things this uses are refused across
it -- ES modules will not load, workers will not start, and fetching the
.wasm is a cross-origin request. The same-origin policy is doing its job,
since a local file could otherwise read its neighbours.

Any static host is enough, GitHub Pages included: it serves .wasm with the
right content type and nothing here needs a back end.

For handing round at a talk there is 'make bundle', which produces
dist/rxenum.html: about 265KB, the WebAssembly inlined as base64, the modules
concatenated into one classic script, and the library on the page's own
thread instead of in a worker. It opens from a file, a memory stick or an
e-mail attachment. The only thing given up is that a long seek blocks the
page while it runs, which for anything a person would type is imperceptible.


